2026-06-23 player competition benchmark — UP-FRONT CPO DESIGN RULINGS (logged per the 2026-06-17 E1 ruling: up-front CPO design approvals are recorded here, not only in the contract/handover). The player benchmark (content_architecture §6; player analog of team #511/#512) was designed across this conversation, the CPO deciding each choice live. PR1 (#559, the metric layer) is built+merged; these rulings govern PR2 (engine + mart) and are recorded so a fresh chat does not re-litigate.
  (D1) per-90 normalization — APPROVED reintroducing per-90 player metrics via the proper catalogue route (the A1 anti-pattern was per-90 invented WITHOUT approval; this is the approved route). CPO: "1. yes".
  (D2) PEERS = position group (GK/DEF/MID/ATT) — CPO: "2. yes". Raw per-90 is position-bound (defender goals/90 ≈ 0), so benchmark within position.
  (D3) METHOD = carry BOTH rank-of-N AND percentile — CPO: "3. ok, readability / intuition for the visitor is important". Large player N makes percentile intuitive; rank stays honest in small leagues.
  (D4) FLOOR = minutes >= 270 (≈3 matches) for all metrics; finishing_efficiency ALSO needs shots_on_target >= 10 (its denominator is shots, not minutes — same floor as the leaderboard finishing board). The CPO drove the floor down from the builder's 720 ("waiting 10 matches seems too long"; "the public assesses after 5 games"), then on the percentile insight to 270 ("I would have suggested 270 minutes") — settled against 2025 distributions (450 too low: cameos like Awoniyi 502min/4g spiked).
  (D5) SCOPE = ALL competitions, NO league-only scoping — CPO insight "because we're percentiles ... we can apply benchmarking for tournaments" (percentiles are descriptive/relative, so tournaments compare players within their own edition; single-match comps fall out below the 270 floor). REVERSED the builder's earlier "scope to leagues" recommendation.
  (D6) NO prev-season fallback — CPO: "the fallback approach (b) is not good ... showing pre-season benchmarks on matchday 9 is confusing". Each competition-season benchmarked on its own data; the live current season fills in honestly as players cross the floor; never relabel last season as this.
  (D7) DIRECTION/INTERPRETATION classified now (ruling "A", detailed in the #559 entry above) — output/quality = higher_better, volume/style = neutral; interpretations player-native for website auto-content.
  (D8) METRIC SET = 13 per-90 + the 5 existing rates; CPO added defensive_actions_per90 + scorer_points_per90 for parity with the team aggregate + the leaderboard's scorer_points ("We need scorer_points_per90 as well").
  LEADERBOARDS-vs-BENCHMARK COHERENCE — CPO chose "(a)": keep them as two DELIBERATE lenses (leaderboards = totals/achievements; benchmark = per-90 standing vs position peers), NOT merged. The same metric can rank differently across them (Golden Boot vs efficiency; fbref carries both). Only alignment: finishing% uses the same floor on both. Leaderboard count boards STAY totals (do not convert to per-90).

2026-06-23 player competition benchmark PR2 (engine + mart) — TWO build-time CPO rulings (premise correction + floor refinement). Both decided live this session before the contract was written; they REFINE the D2/D4 design above on facts established from the live warehouse (the "establish facts from the source of record before proposing" rule).
  (B1) POSITION SOURCE = season-level position_code from fct_fixture_player_stats, NOT dim_player.player_position — CPO: "B". PREMISE CORRECTION: D2/the handover recorded peers "from dim_player.player_position; ~0.5% null among 270-min qualifiers". Querying the live table showed the 0.5% holds ONLY for 2025; dim_player.player_position is a CURRENT-bio snapshot (latest /players/profiles), so historical qualifiers lose their position — null is 0.1% (2026), 0.5% (2025), 5.6% (2024), 18% (2022), 35% (2019), 50% (2017), 58% (2016). Under D5 (all seasons / deep backfill) that would silently drop ~40-58% of old-season benchmarks. fct_fixture_player_stats.position_code is recorded AT MATCH TIME (G/D/M/F -> GK/DEF/MID/ATT): 100% coverage at the 270-min qualifier grain in EVERY season 2016-2026 (0 null), and season-accurate (the role the player actually played that season, not a current-bio tag). The position-group CONCEPT (D2) is unchanged; only the source changes.
  (B2) FLOOR = 270 minutes IN THE POSITION (per position_code), which both QUALIFIES and ASSIGNS — no modal/mode step — CPO: "We already have a rule, it's >= 270 minutes. Now it means in this position" / "Yes, I agree". The benchmark VALUE grain becomes per (player, season, position): the per-90 a player produced IN that role, built from the per-fixture legs grouped by position_code — NOT the #559 whole-season per-90 (which stays the profile metric). ~10% of qualifiers clear 270 in two positions (6,561 of ~67,003) and appear in BOTH benchmarks, each on their in-role per-90 (honest; modal would have polluted one pool with the other role's stats). finishing_efficiency also needs shots_on_target >= 10 IN the position (D4 carried through).
  (B3) METRIC ELIGIBILITY PER POSITION = Path B (per-position relevant set), NOT compute-all-x-all — CPO: "I agree" (the CPO flagged this was decided for the TEAM benchmark — curate-at-macro + show-subset-at-display, but the curation was GLOBAL because team metrics aren't position-bound — and was NOT decided for players). Player metrics ARE position-bound (live data: 100% of GKs record a save, 0.0% of any outfield group; 81% ATT / 55% MID / 32% DEF score vs 0.3% of GKs), so compute-all-x-all yields degenerate boards (every outfielder tied at saves/90=0; GKs ~0 on attacking). RULE: benchmark a metric for a position only when non-degenerate; because the peer pool is already position-specific the only dead boards are at the GK<->outfield boundary, so eligibility is essentially binary. MAP (CPO-approved): GK = {saves_per90, save_pct, passes_per90, pass_accuracy_pct} (4 — GK distribution is a real fbref-style axis); DEF = MID = ATT = the other 16. NO finer DEF/MID/ATT split (the peer pool already makes within-outfield comparisons position-relative; e.g. a CB's goals_per90 is ranked only vs other CBs, surfacing attacking CBs honestly; splitting would bake DISPLAY into the benchmark). 52 position x metric boards, not 72. Eligibility lives as a per-metric position list in player_benchmark_metrics() (shared engine<->mart). RESERVED (still §10, downstream): which of a position's ELIGIBLE metrics a PAGE surfaces + their order = page-composition.

2026-06-25 #567 (PR-a metric-layer naming) rebased onto main after #569 (finishing open-play) merged — football-analytics ESCALATE: the merged "team and player" finishing_efficiency catalogue row has one description whose null clause is team-centric ("Null when the window is not fully shot-covered"); for players the current code nulls only when there are no shots on goal. CPO ANSWER (this conversation): the incomplete-data->NULL rule is UNIVERSAL — every metric, team and player, is NULL when its inputs are not 100% available. The description states the correct INTENDED rule for both entities, so no reword. The actual defect is the player IMPLEMENTATION: it coalesces missing per-game stats to 0 and computes anyway (e.g. int_player_season__metrics `sum(coalesce(shots_on, 0))`), and the team per-match rates average over only the covered games (the older #320 design) rather than nulling on partial coverage. Bringing EVERY metric (team + player) into compliance with the universal rule — NULL whenever any game in the window is missing the stat, replacing coalesce-to-0 and average-over-covered-games — is a separate CPO-directed task (changes shipped numbers broadly; own before/after deltas + review). It is NOT part of #567, which is rename-only and inherits today's behaviour.

2026-06-25 fix/incomplete-data-null — universal incomplete-data->NULL task. The RULE is LOCKED (every metric, team AND player, is NULL when its inputs are not 100% available across the window; recorded 2026-06-25 above). Two REACH/CONSEQUENCE questions raised BLINDED (working_agreement §11, two paths each, no recommendation) BEFORE writing any model — they set the blast radius, not the rule. Both put to the CPO in plain language (AskUserQuestion, 2026-06-25):
  (Q1 — reach to counts/per-90s) Does the rule apply to COUNT totals (a player's season goals/shots/tackles that drive the leaderboards; any team stat-sum totals shown as counts) and PER-90s too, or only to per-match averages + ratios? Path A = include counts + per-90s (maximally consistent; any entity with one uncovered window game drops off the count leaderboards / loses its per-90s — boards can be sparse early-season / low-coverage). Path B = only averages + ratios (counts/per-90s stay honest best-effort sums; leaderboards stay populated but totals can understate uncovered players + aren't strictly comparable).
      CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-06-25): **Path A — include counts + per-90s.** Every stats-based metric NULLs on any uncovered window game; count totals and per-90s included. The leaderboard drop-off (handled by mart_leaderboards' existing `where <metric> > 0`, which excludes a NULL count) is the accepted, intended consequence.
  (Q2 — #320 reversal) Confirm this deliberately REVERSES the #320 "average over the covered games (+ caption)" design for the TEAM per-match rates (they go NULL on any partial coverage instead of averaging over the covered subset). Path A = reverse #320 (consistent; partial-window team averages become '—'; moves live numbers). Path B = keep #320 for team rates (stays populated but the codebase keeps two incompleteness rules — the inconsistency this task removes).
      CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-06-25): **Path A — reverse #320.** Team per-match rates NULL on partial coverage; this is a deliberate, recorded reversal of the #320 same-window-average design (the marts today divide each rate by its coverage count — games_with_team_stats / games_with_sot_stats / games_with_opp_stats / games_with_player_stats — and the change ALSO gates: NULL when that count < the window total). Scoreline-based metrics (goals/points/W-D-L/clean sheets) stay always-covered → never NULL from this rule.

2026-06-25 fix/incomplete-data-null — PREMISE CORRECTION (player side) + CPO ruling, BEFORE any model code. The spec said the player models "coalesce missing per-game stats to 0 and compute anyway" and prescribed adding per-stat coverage counts (countif(field is not null)) gated vs appearances. A source-of-record check (read-only BQ, per Appendix A6 "measure before asserting") REFUTED that premise:
  - Player stat nulls predominantly mean ZERO, not missing: of 1.29M player rows with minutes>0, 91.7% have null goals_total; saves null 95.1% overall. (Team stat nulls, by contrast, are 0.6-3.7% → genuinely missing.)
  - PROOF via ground truth (the scoreline): summing player goals_total (null→0) per team-fixture reconciles EXACTLY with the actual match score in 95.88% of the 85,738 finished team-fixtures that have player data (~all the rest off by exactly 1 = own goals, which aren't in any player's goals_total). If null meant "missing", this reconciliation would be near-zero. So null=zero is proven; each recorded player appearance is COMPLETE and coalesce-to-0 is correct.
  - Concrete example: a Liverpool 4-goal match — Mané 2 + Salah 1 + Sturridge 1 = 4 = score; every other player's goals = null while playing ~90 min with full passing data (null = didn't score, not missing).
  - The genuine player incompleteness is FIXTURE-LEVEL: 21,246 of 106,984 finished team-fixtures (19.9%) have NO player data at all.
  So the spec's per-FIELD player coverage gate would NULL ~92% of players (empty leaderboards) — invalid. Put to the CPO with the evidence + the concrete example (no anchoring).
      CPO RULING (conversation, 2026-06-25): "So for player treat NULL as zero is correct? Do it." → the PLAYER models are LEFT AS-IS (null=zero / coalesce-to-0 is correct); the per-field player gate is DROPPED. The ~20%-of-matches fixture-level player-data gap is LEFT ALONE (not handled in this task). The TEAM side proceeds unchanged (team stat nulls genuinely = missing): reverse #320 — NULL team per-match rates + stat-count totals + the team player-DERIVED per-match metrics (key_passes/tackles/duels — these inherit the fixture-level player-data gap, tracked by games_with_player_stats) on partial coverage. Q1 (include counts + per-90s) now applies to the TEAM stat-count totals only. The task is therefore TEAM-ONLY.

2026-06-25 refactor/500-pr-b — #500 PR-b sliced + a §10 naming-collision ruling. (1) SLICE: the CPO ("go ahead as recommended") split PR-b into PR-b1 = the deep ATOM renames first (goals_saves->saves, goals_conceded->goals_against, shots_on_target->shots_on_goal end-to-end), with the model FILE renames (int_<entity>_<window>__metrics) + the _season-suffix drop + team-season consolidation DEFERRED to a second slice once the file-rename MAPPING is pinned (genuinely §10 + underspecified: do the momentum builders rename? do int_season_record__*/the position model rename?). (2) COLLISION (§10 naming, surfaced during PR-b1 scoping): in mart_player_match_log the locked player rename goals_conceded->goals_against clashes with an EXISTING goals_against = the team match-scoreline (the team's goals conceded that match, used for W/D/L) — two distinct concepts, two goals_against columns in one SELECT. Established from the source of record that the player goals_conceded is the GOALKEEPER's conceded (in a sample match only the keeper carried the value=2; all 10 outfielders=0; saves are GK-only too), which for a full-match keeper EQUALS the team match goals_against. Builder first suggested inventing team_goals_for/team_goals_against; CPO flagged that introduces a NEW metric (a §10 invention) — withdrawn. Builder then recommended dropping the redundant player column (in a per-match log the match goals_against already shows what was conceded; the player figure differs only for subbed keepers).
      CPO RULING (conversation, 2026-06-25): "drop the redundant player goals_conceded column from mart_player_match_log." -> mart_player_match_log DROPS the player goals_conceded column (keeps the keeper's saves [from goals_saves->saves] + the unchanged match goals_against). No new identifier invented; the player goals_against metric still lives in the player season/momentum models + the catalogue (paired with saves for save_pct). This is the ONLY file with the collision; all other files apply the locked rename straight.

