## 2026-08-03 — #897, production pauses between API calls (PR 1 of the ingest silent-failure cluster)

- (CPO ruling, AskUserQuestion, taken as ONE batch before any file was touched) Four questions were
  put together rather than one per round, and all four were answered in a single pass:
  1. **#897 is fixed via the `settings.py` profile default, not via `dbt-scheduled.yml`.** The
     workflow route touches a protected path and would need `protected_override` + `impact_map` +
     cto/platform at the opus floor. The settings route needs none of that AND prevents recurrence,
     because after it nothing can inherit zero pacing by omission. The value is 250 ms, the figure
     `docs/api_football_ingestion_blueprint.md` §4 already mandates.
  2. **#898's threshold policy: visible always, fail only on STAGNATION.** Dropped calls are
     surfaced in the job summary counted by endpoint on every run; a single bad run never fails,
     because failing blocks the dbt build and daily freshness is a stated non-negotiable. The run
     fails only when the same entity drops on N consecutive runs, i.e. when it is demonstrably not
     healing. Reuses the existing `stagnant_statistics` no-progress-since-last-run pattern rather
     than inventing a mechanism. Belongs to PR 3, recorded here because it was decided here.
  3. **#896 covers the empty AND the partial case in one PR.** The CPO classified
     "a fetch result carries whether it is complete, and only a complete result may supersede" as an
     extension of the `quota_cut` PARTIAL handling already in `squads.py`, NOT a new mechanism.
  4. **Three sequential PRs**, #897 then #896 then #898, so the one-setting pacing fix can merge
     without waiting on the other two.

- (⭐ THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLED THE COST APPROVAL, and it corrects a doc we wrote) The blueprint's
  §4 table had **no row for the plan we are actually on** and its pacing rule was written against
  Pro's 300/min. Measured from the provider's own response headers: `x-ratelimit-limit: 450` and
  `x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 75000`. We are on **Ultra: 450/min, 75,000/day**. So 250 ms caps the
  request rate at 240/min, i.e. **53% of the real ceiling**, not the 80% the old table implied.
  Daily usage measured the same day: **7,437 of 75,000, about 10%**. This CONFIRMS the two prior
  signed-off assessments that the API budget is fine, and is the evidence the standing rule demanded
  before anyone claims otherwise.

- (the wall-clock trade, approved with the number in hand) Pacing is not free and the cost is
  RECURRING. Ingest averages **0.455 s/call** today (63 min of ingest, 06:32:20 to 07:35:18 on the
  2026-08-02 nightly); adding 250 ms makes it 0.705 s/call, a factor of **1.55**. Ingest goes to
  ~98 min and the whole run from 1h11m to about **1h46m**. The job sets no `timeout-minutes`, so
  GitHub's 6h default applies: **~3.4x headroom**, and Actions minutes are free on a public repo.
  Daily freshness therefore survives, which is the non-negotiable this trade was checked against.
  Cost, data quality and daily freshness are all non-negotiable, so trading one for another is a CPO
  call; the numbers were put in front of the CPO before the option was chosen, not after.

- (SCOPE DRIFT, caught and REVERTED by the builder before commit) The approved plan listed the
  blueprint's stale "20-50 API calls total" estimate under **Not in scope**. While correcting the
  §4 table I edited that bullet anyway, having first added a measured "~8,300 calls" figure directly
  above it that made the two adjacent lines contradict. Both edits were reverted so the diff matches
  the approved plan exactly, and the stale estimate was filed as **#900** instead. ⚠ THE LESSON is
  the one already in this log in a different form: when an edit outside the agreed scope looks
  obviously right, the drift is not the edit, it is having created the justification for it a moment
  earlier. The plan is the authority, and "it is only one line" is how a contract stops meaning
  anything.

- (the gate corrected the contract, and it was right) The first contract said "No structural path in
  scope, so no `impact_map`". The CONTRACT GATE refused the `settings.py` edit: that file is on the
  structural surface. The `impact_map` was added on a clean tree as an evidenced SHORT FORM, and the
  evidence is a complete call trace rather than a claim of triviality: `API_FOOTBALL_REQUEST_PAUSE_MS`
  is read at exactly one site (`quota.py:99`), which has exactly one caller (`_throttle`,
  `quota.py:109`), which has exactly one call site (`http_client.py:51`). No dbt node, no schema, no
  column, no row grain is touched, so there is no lineage to trace and no layer rule engaged. The
  reachable effect of the diff is the duration of one `time.sleep()`.

- (a test that pins behaviour, because #892 is the precedent) #897 asked for a test pinning the
  default, since a silently reverted default regressed for two months. `tests/test_ingest_profile_pacing.py`
  pins the effective pause through `_request_pause_seconds()`, not just the env string, so a change
  to either `settings.py` or `quota.py` has to face it. **Verified it actually pins**: reverted the
  value to `0` and 4 of 7 tests failed, including `test_production_config_is_paced`, which
  reproduces production exactly (no profile set, so `full` is inherited). The 3 that still passed
  are the explicit-override and economy cases, which correctly do not depend on the default.

- (⚠ A BUG IN MY OWN TEST, found by running the FULL suite rather than the new file) The new tests
  passed alone and broke `test_wide_history_truncates_to_profile_cap_on_default_profile` when the
  suite ran in file order. Cause: `_apply_ingest_profile_defaults` writes the WHOLE profile bundle
  via `os.environ.setdefault` (`ALL_SEASONS`, page caps, fanout soft cap), and **monkeypatch only
  reverts what monkeypatch itself set**, so the bundle leaked into later tests. Fixed with an
  autouse fixture that snapshots and restores `os.environ` wholesale. THE LESSON: a test that calls
  a function which mutates global process state cannot be cleaned up with `monkeypatch.delenv` on
  the one variable under test, and running only the new file will never show it.

## 2026-08-03 — #896, an incomplete fetch must never supersede good data (PR 2 of the cluster)

- (⭐ THE CPO DECISION THAT SHAPED THE FIX, taken with the measurement in hand) The question put was
  whether an EMPTY-but-error-free response counts as incomplete. It is a cost-versus-data-quality
  trade, which §10 reserves to the CPO, so it was escalated with both paths priced rather than
  decided by the builder. **Ruling: THE TRIGGER IS THE ERROR SIGNAL, NOT EMPTINESS.** A fetch does
  not supersede when it carried a body-level error or was cut by the quota flag mid-pagination. An
  empty response with NO error is the provider genuinely reporting no players, and DOES supersede.
  - The rejected alternative is what #896's own text suggests ("skips the delete when the new
    payload has zero entities"). It was rejected on a measured price:
    **4,973 of 22,589 team-season blocks in `RAW_APIF_PLAYERS` already hold an empty payload, and
    3,539 of those are historical.** Refusing to WRITE them keeps them out of
    `captured_player_team_seasons` forever, so `plan_player_team_season_fetch` re-fetches all 3,539
    EVERY night. At 0.705 s/call that is about **+42 minutes per run, permanently**, taking the
    nightly from ~1h46m to roughly 2h28m.
  - The evidence that the cheaper option suffices: **all four measured loss cases carried a
    body-level error** (UCL 340, UEL 573, UECL 20034, APD 463 all appear in the 2026-08-02 run's
    rate-limit list), so the error signal catches every case actually observed.
  - KNOWN GAP, stated rather than hidden: an empty response with no error is indistinguishable from
    a genuinely empty squad and would still supersede. Accepted deliberately.

- (the trap that would have broken the cheap option, found by reading `fetch_json` rather than
  assuming) `fetch_json` returns `{"errors": [], "response": [], ...}` when
  `_http_quota_exhausted` is already set — an empty body with **NO error**. So "error signal only",
  implemented naively, would have read a quota-exhausted call as a genuine empty and deleted good
  rows on exactly the run where the most calls fail. `complete` therefore combines BOTH signals:
  no body-level error AND no quota cut. This is why the fix lives in `fetch_merged_paged`, where
  both are visible, rather than in the loader.

- (why the WRITE is withheld and not just the DELETE) `captured_player_team_seasons` reads
  `$.team_id`/`$.season` from the stored block, and those are present even when `players_payload`
  is `[]`. Writing an empty row therefore marks the team-season CAPTURED, and
  `plan_player_team_season_fetch` skips any historical season already captured. Writing the failed
  row would have converted a transient rate limit into a permanent hole that never re-fetches.
  Skipping both the write and the delete is what makes the failure self-heal on the next clean run.

- (ROOT CAUSE, confirmed against the models rather than inferred) `base_apif__player_team_season`
  unnests per player and picks with `qualify row_number() ... order by raw_ingested_at desc`. An
  empty payload contributes ZERO rows, so it cannot win that race; it is not in the race at all.
  The downstream loss is caused SOLELY by the raw row being physically deleted. That is also why
  `transfers` behaves differently: `load_transfers_batch` is append-only with no delete, so its raw
  history survives and the gap is staging masking rather than destruction.

- (⭐ THE HANDOVER ITEM THAT WAS UNVERIFIED IS NOW OBSERVED) The handover recorded transfers healing
  as INFERRED and NOT OBSERVED. Measured on `RAW_APIF_TRANSFERS` for UEL team 376:
  **184 players on 08-01, 0 on 08-02, 184 again on 08-03.** Four earlier dip-and-recover cycles
  appear in the same series (07-16 86,232 to 07-17 94,168; 07-19 91,004 to 07-20 94,719; 07-21
  90,844 to 07-22 94,836). Healing is now OBSERVED, not inferred, on the exact team the handover
  flagged.

- (scope) The merge-on-write pattern exists in exactly ONE place. `grep -rn "delete from|_delete_superseded"`
  over `ingestion/` returns three lines, all inside `loads/squads.py`, so there is no second site to
  fix and the issue's "wherever the merge-on-write pattern is reused" resolves to nothing further.

- (pre-existing defect NOTED and deliberately NOT fixed) `fetch_merged_paged` builds
  `out = dict(meta)` where `meta` stays None if the page loop breaks on its first iteration, which
  would raise TypeError. Not reachable from `load_squad_players_batch`, which guards on the quota
  flag before calling. Left alone rather than folded in, per scope discipline.

- (verification) The tests pin BOTH halves, because pinning only the refusal would let a fix that
  never supersedes anything pass. Confirmed they pin: with the guard disabled, the 4 "must not
  supersede" tests fail and the 7 that cover successful superseding plus the completeness signal
  itself correctly still pass. Full suite 525 passed.

- (⭐ ROUND 1 FAILED, and the finding is the most valuable thing in this task) `data-engineer-reviewer`
  FAILED the first version. The fix carried the completeness signal as a NEW KEY on the dict
  returned by `fetch_merged_paged`. That function is shared by seven call sites, and FOUR loaders
  build the raw payload they persist by copying every key except a fixed exclusion list. The key
  would therefore have been physically written into **four raw tables**: `RAW_APIF_STANDINGS` (via
  `seasons.py::_merge_merged_paged`, `loads/standings.py:47-55`), `RAW_APIF_TEAMS`
  (`loads/teams.py:41-43`), `RAW_APIF_INJURIES` (`loads/injuries.py:52-58`) and
  `RAW_APIF_FIXTURES_NEXT` (`loads/fixtures.py:242-249`). `load_json_to_bq` stores `payload` as a
  schemaless JSON column with no key filtering, so nothing would have stripped it.
  - **The value would also have been WRONG, not merely extra.** `_merge_merged_paged` copies from
    the FIRST source only and never refreshes the key on later iterations, so it would freeze at
    the first season of a multi-season loop: a run whose fourth season was rate-limited would still
    have recorded `"complete": true` for the whole merged snapshot.
  - The reviewer also noted the word collides with `docs/data_contract.md:61`, where "complete
    snapshot" already names the load-bearing append-only invariant staging depends on. A future
    reader of those payloads could have read a stale per-iteration flag as that invariant.
  - RESOLUTION: completeness became a FUNCTION, `http_client.result_is_complete`. No returned dict
    gains a key, so the leak is impossible BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by remembering to add an
    exclusion in four separate places. A regression test pins the returned key set, and it was
    verified to work by reintroducing the key and watching
    `TestReturnedKeySetIsStable::test_non_paginated_returns_only_contract_keys` fail with
    "Extra items in the left set: 'complete'". Full suite after the redesign: 528 passed.
  - ⚠ THE LESSON, and it generalises past this task: **the builder had already found two of the four
    leak sites and still under-called it.** I checked `standings` and `teams`, concluded "a real
    defect", and planned the fix; the reviewer found `injuries` and `fixtures` as well, plus the
    frozen-value bug, which is the part that would have made the data actively misleading rather
    than merely noisy. Finding a defect yourself is not evidence you have found all of it. The
    `impact_map` was the specific thing at fault: it traced the ONE table the task was about and
    stopped, when the diff edited a helper shared by seven callers. When a diff touches a shared
    low-level function, the impact map owes every caller, not the caller you came for.

## 2026-08-03 — #898, a run that drops API calls cannot look clean (PR 3, closes the cluster)

- (CPO ruling, recorded when the batch was taken before PR 1, implemented here) THE THRESHOLD POLICY
  IS **VISIBLE ALWAYS, FAIL ONLY ON STAGNATION**. Dropped calls are counted by endpoint and shown in
  the job summary on EVERY run. A single bad run never fails, because failing blocks the dbt build
  and daily freshness is a stated non-negotiable. The run fails only when the SAME endpoint drops on
  consecutive runs, i.e. when it is demonstrably not healing. It reuses the existing
  `stagnant_statistics` no-progress-since-last-run pattern and its existing snapshot table, which
  was the explicit instruction: do not invent a mechanism.

- (N settled by rule, not escalated) N = 2 consecutive runs. `detect_stagnant_statistics_backfill`
  already compares exactly prior-versus-current, and both gates now share ONE exit path, so a second
  stagnation signal on that path uses the same window. Two gates on one exit code disagreeing about
  what "stagnant" means would be a defect, not a choice. Per the standing rule, a question a rule
  can settle is not put to the CPO.

- (⚠ THE FRESHNESS CONSEQUENCE, traced in the workflow rather than assumed) A hard fail returns 503,
  which `main.py` maps to exit code 3. In `dbt-scheduled.yml` every post-ingest step is conditioned
  on `steps.ingest.outputs.new_data == 'true'` with **no `always()`**, and GitHub Actions ANDs an
  implicit `success()` into that. So a nonzero ingest exit SKIPS `dbt deps`, `dbt seed`, the
  layer-contract check and `dbt build`: on the day the gate fires, the marts are a day stale. This
  is not new behaviour introduced here (the existing `stagnant_statistics` gate already exits the
  same way); this diff adds a second trigger to the same path. Stated explicitly because it trades
  daily freshness, which is CPO territory, and the CPO approved the trade knowing a fail blocks the
  build.

- (the retry is BEST EFFORT and is documented as such rather than oversold) `fetch_json` now gives
  the per-minute limit the single retry a 429 already got, because it IS a 429 that the provider
  chose to deliver as HTTP 200 with the error in the body. That is why `raise_for_status()` passed
  and the call was dropped with ZERO retries. But a per-minute window can take up to 60s to clear
  and the retry waits `Retry-After` or 3s, so it will not rescue every call. Waiting out a full
  minute per drop was considered and rejected: it trades run time for a case #897's pacing should
  already have made rare. The guarantees live elsewhere — #897 makes limits rare, #896 makes a drop
  non-destructive, and this makes what remains impossible to miss.

- (the ordering bug avoided) The prior dropped-call counts are read BEFORE this run's are persisted.
  Reading after would compare the run against itself and every run would look stagnant. The read and
  the write both use the existing `COMPLETENESS_SNAPSHOT_TABLE`.

- (⭐ THE #896 LESSON APPLIED, and it is why this contract's impact_map looks different) #896 round 1
  failed because the impact map traced the one table the task was about while the diff edited a
  helper shared by seven callers. This task edits TWO shared functions, so both were enumerated
  before writing a line:
  - `append_api_errors` has **16 call sites** (`grep -rn "append_api_errors(" ingestion/`), and every
    one passes a context whose FIRST token is the endpoint name. That is what makes counting by
    endpoint possible without touching a single call site, and the change adds a counter increment
    only: the return value, the messages appended to the sink, and their order are untouched.
  - `fetch_json` is the single HTTP entry point for every endpoint. The new branch adds no key to
    the returned dict, which is the constraint #896 established, and #896's
    `TestReturnedKeySetIsStable` would catch a regression.
  - The snapshot table gains a key, so its consumers were checked rather than assumed:
    `grep -rn "INGEST_COMPLETENESS_SNAPSHOT" dbt_project/ scripts/` returns NOTHING and no dbt
    source or model references it. It is internal run state, so the extra key has zero downstream
    blast radius. That is precisely the check whose absence caused #896 round 1.

- (the dangerous flag, guarded by a test) `_payload_shows_minute_rate_limit` must NEVER set
  `_http_quota_exhausted`. That flag stops all remaining HTTP for the run, which is right for a
  daily cap that will not recover and catastrophic for a limit that clears within the minute. There
  is a test asserting the detector leaves it false, and another asserting the two detectors do not
  overlap in either direction.

- (verification) 19 new tests, full suite 547 passed. Confirmed they pin: disabling the retry branch
  fails `test_body_level_limit_is_retried_once` with "a per-minute limit must be retried" and
  `test_retry_is_attempted_only_once`, while the 17 covering detection, counting and the stagnation
  window correctly still pass because they do not depend on the retry.

- (⭐ ROUND 1 FAILED TWICE, on two independent defects, and both were mine)

  **FAIL A, `data-engineer-reviewer`: the counter double-counted `fixtures`.** The first version
  counted inside `append_api_errors`, keyed off the caller's context string, justified in the
  contract by "all 16 call sites pass the endpoint as the first token". That audit treated all 16
  grep hits as equivalent single-fetch sites. They are not: `loads/fixtures.py` calls it TWICE
  against OVERLAPPING data, per season at :163 and again at :183 on the accumulated envelope, and
  `seasons.py::_merge_merged_paged` does `dst["errors"].extend(...)` so the earlier seasons'
  rate-limit text is carried forward into the post-loop call. One real drop, counted twice.
  Verified independently by reading those lines before accepting the finding.
  - It did not corrupt the pass/fail boolean (`detect_stagnant_dropped_calls` tests `count > 0`,
    not magnitude) but it corrupted THE NUMBER, which is the entire deliverable of #898: after
    three weeks of runs whose success meant nothing, the fix shipped a headline count that was
    structurally wrong for its most-called endpoint.
  - RESOLUTION: counting moved to `fetch_json`, once per HTTP call whose FINAL attempt was still
    rejected, keyed by API path. `append_api_errors` is now unchanged. This is fix-the-class rather
    than fix-the-instance: the tally no longer depends on how many times any caller reports the
    same error, so no future caller shape can inflate it. A retry that SUCCEEDS is deliberately not
    counted, because no data was lost. Guard test verified by reinstating the old counting, which
    fails with `{'fixtures': 2} == {}` — the defect reproduced exactly.

  **FAIL B, `platform-reviewer`: the new gate bypassed both operator kill-switches.**
  `stagnant_dropped` was computed in the orchestrator and ORed into the exit condition, so it
  honoured neither `report["skipped"]` (`API_FOOTBALL_SKIP_COMPLETENESS_CHECK`) nor
  `fail_on_incomplete()` (`API_FOOTBALL_FAIL_ON_INCOMPLETE=0`). The pre-existing
  `stagnant_statistics` signal honours both, because it lives inside
  `evaluate_completeness_outcome`. So an operator following the documented backfill playbook in
  `docs/operations_guide.md` would still have got exit 3 — and a backfill is heavy on calls, which
  is exactly when this fires. Verified against `completeness.py`'s early return and its
  `fail_on_incomplete()` condition before accepting.
  - ⚠ It contradicted THIS TASK'S OWN CONTRACT, which argued N must match the existing gate because
    "two gates on one exit code disagreeing about what stagnant means would be a defect". I applied
    that reasoning to the WINDOW and not to WHEN THE GATE APPLIES AT ALL, which is the larger of
    the two disagreements.
  - RESOLUTION: the signal moved INTO `evaluate_completeness_outcome`, so both stagnation signals
    are now subject to the same early return and the same `fail_on_incomplete()` condition, by
    construction rather than by duplicated checks. The orchestrator reads
    `outcome["stagnant_dropped_calls"]` and its exit condition is back to plain
    `if outcome["hard_fail"]`. Visibility is unaffected: the "DROPPED n call(s)" note is
    independent of the report and still prints on every run, so the escape hatches suppress the
    FAILURE and never the REPORTING.

  ⚠ THE LESSON, and it is the second time in two tasks: **both defects were in the part of the
  contract I wrote most confidently.** FAIL A was justified by a grep whose 16 hits I counted but
  did not read; FAIL B was justified by a consistency argument I applied to one dimension and not
  the obvious second one. An enumeration is not an audit until each item is read, and a consistency
  argument has to be run against every axis it names, not the first.

- (an existing guard extended, never loosened) `test_skipped_report_returns_no_failures` asserts
  `evaluate_completeness_outcome`'s returned dict by EXACT equality, and the new key broke it. The
  expected dict was extended to include `stagnant_dropped_calls`, deliberately NOT relaxed to a
  subset check, so a future signal still cannot be added there unnoticed. That file was outside
  `scope_paths` and the CONTRACT GATE refused the edit; the contract was amended on a clean tree via
  the stash-dance, admitting that one test file. The first question asked was whether the EDIT
  belongs in this branch rather than how to widen the contract: it does, because the approved change
  breaks that test and the branch cannot be green without it.

- (verification, round 2) Full suite 553 passed.

## 2026-08-03 — cause 3: the per-team endpoints get a completeness check

- (⭐ THE PREMISE WAS CHECKED BEFORE THE WORK, and it changed what the task is) The CPO picked cause
  3 over cost on my ranking. I then measured it, per §11's premise-check duty, and brought the
  numbers back BEFORE spending a cycle: **transfers 0 teams missing, player_squads 0, players 0 once
  each competition's own reference season is used, coaches 22-26 of 1,265 and STABLE across five
  days (07-30 to 08-03).** The per-team data is COMPLETE. So this is regression insurance, not
  repair. The CPO confirmed on exactly that basis, and the basis is sound: a gate introduced while
  the metric reads 0 starts green and can only ever fire on a real regression, which is the safest
  moment to add one.

- (CPO ruling, one batch, before any file was touched)
  1. Build it now, as insurance.
  2. **GATE players, squads, transfers. REPORT ONLY coaches.** ~23 teams have no coach on every
     single run because the provider genuinely has none; gating that is permanently red, and a
     permanently-red gate trains everyone to ignore the alarm, which is precisely how ten green runs
     came to mean nothing.
  3. **Expected is per competition, never a global year.**

- (⚠ TWO EXPECTED-SET DESIGNS WERE WRONG, and BOTH were caught by running against production rather
  than by reasoning. This is the single most useful thing in this entry.)
  1. **Hardcoding the calendar year.** My first measurement reported ACN as 24 of 24 teams missing.
     ACN is on season **2027**, and so is J1. The pipeline was right and my query was wrong. A gate
     shipping that assumption is permanently red on every tournament.
  2. **Deriving the league set from `RAW_APIF_TEAMS`.** Running the real function against production
     judged **45 leagues, not 26**, and reported **33 league/entity pairs missing, including
     FAC/PLAYERS 749**. Restricting to `selected_competitions()` did NOT fix it: that also returns
     45. The reason is POLL MODE — those competitions are selected but run `run_poll_phases`, which
     never fetches teams, players, squads or transfers. Nothing was missing; nothing had ever been
     fetched.
  - RESOLUTION, and it is better than either attempt: **expected comes from the RUN, not from
    BigQuery.** The orchestrator already holds `results`, a list of `CompetitionRunResult`, and only
    competitions that ran the full phases are in it. `team_ids` is the exact set the loaders
    iterated and `max(seasons_list)` is the exact value `load_squad_players_batch` was called with,
    so expectation and fetch cannot drift apart by construction. It is also free: no teams query at
    all.
  - ⚠ THE LESSON: I would have shipped a gate that fired on 33 pairs on its first run, having
    "verified" the design with a query that agreed with me. The plan's own verification step said to
    run it against production and that anything other than 0 means THE EXPECTED SET is wrong, not
    the data. That step is what caught it. Write that step into the plan every time.

- (⭐ THE COST SHAPE, measured, and the documented trap reproduced exactly) `RAW_APIF_TRANSFERS` is
  6.99 GiB, so the read shape matters more than the read. Dry-run:
    - `WHERE ingested_at = (SELECT MAX(...))`  **7.51 GB** — a subquery predicate does NOT prune
    - `WHERE date(ingested_at) IN (<literals>)` **384 MB**
    - `SELECT league_code, MAX(ingested_at)` alone **14.8 KB**
  So the read is deliberately TWO steps: cheap maxima first, then literals inlined. That is 19.6x on
  our largest raw table, and a future reader will want to collapse it into one query. A fixed
  lookback window is NOT an alternative and the standing rule says so: poll-mode competitions go
  months between refreshes (WC last 2026-07-20, CWC 2026-06-22), so a recent-days filter reports
  those leagues as 100% missing.

- (the kill-switch lesson applied UP FRONT rather than after a FAIL) `stagnant_per_team_gaps` is
  computed INSIDE `evaluate_completeness_outcome`, so it inherits both operator escape hatches by
  construction: the `report["skipped"]` early return and `fail_on_incomplete()`. The previous task
  shipped the equivalent signal ORed into the orchestrator and `platform-reviewer` FAILED it,
  because a documented backfill would still have exited 3. Same class, fixed before review this
  time, and pinned by four tests.

- (the stagnation window, settled by rule not escalated) Two consecutive runs, matching
  `detect_stagnant_statistics_backfill` and `detect_stagnant_dropped_calls`. Three stagnation
  signals now share one exit code and must agree on what "stagnant" means. The CPO's freshness
  reasoning for #898 applies unchanged: a hard fail skips `dbt build`, so a transient must not
  trigger it, and a brand-new team whose first fetch fails would otherwise fail the run on the day
  it appears.

- (an existing guard EXTENDED, never loosened) `test_skipped_report_returns_no_failures` asserts the
  outcome dict by exact equality. The new key was added to the literal. `tests/test_completeness_outcome_and_summary.py`
  was declared in `scope_paths` FROM THE START this time, rather than amended in mid-task, which is
  what the previous task had to do after the gate refused the edit.

- (verification) Against production with a run-shaped expectation set: 26 leagues judged,
  **PLAYERS 0, SQUADS 0, TRANSFERS 0 missing, COACHES 29**, and the GATED set is EMPTY, so the gate
  starts green exactly as the premise required. ACN resolves to reference season 2027 with 0 players
  missing. 20 new tests, full suite **573 passed**. Confirmed the guard pins: adding COACHES to
  `PER_TEAM_GATED` fails three tests with `{'BL1/COACHES': 7}` leaking into the gate.

- (⭐ ROUND 1 FAILED ON BOTH SPECIALISTS, and the second finding is the sharper one)

  **FAIL A, found by BOTH reviewers independently: PLAYERS had no table-existence guard.** The three
  snapshot entities get one for free via `_latest_snapshot_timestamps`, which does
  `get_table`/`NotFound`. PLAYERS has no maxima step and so had none, and the contract's
  `impact_map` claimed the function "mirrors `coverage.read_coverage`, including that function's
  `client.get_table` / `NotFound` guard". That was true for three of four blocks. An absent table
  would raise through the orchestrator's blanket handler and hard-fail the run on the FIRST
  occurrence, skipping `dbt build` — the precise outcome the two-run stagnation window exists to
  prevent. `data-engineer-reviewer` also noted it is operationally reachable, not theoretical:
  `API_FOOTBALL_SKIP_PLAYERS` is a documented archive-run flag. A second, related defect: with no
  blocks at all the UNION rendered `FROM ()`, invalid SQL, executed unguarded.
  RESOLUTION: `_raw_table_exists` guards PLAYERS, and the query is skipped entirely when no block
  survives.

  **FAIL B, `data-engineer-reviewer` only, and it is the one I should have seen: the snapshot
  timestamp was not paired to its own league.** `_snapshot_block` applied
  `ingested_at IN (<all leagues' maxima>)` and `league_code IN (<all leagues>)` as two INDEPENDENT
  filters, so a row for league A carrying league B's latest timestamp satisfied both and counted as
  coverage for A. It fails in the DANGEROUS direction: it makes a real miss look covered, which is
  the exact property this whole task exists to guarantee.
  ⚠ THE LESSON: I had already written the comment explaining why pairing matters — for the PLAYERS
  season list, three lines away — and did not apply the same reasoning to the timestamps. Recognising
  a bug class and fixing one instance of it is not the same as sweeping for it. This is the
  fix-the-class-not-the-instance rule turned on its author.
  RESOLUTION: `(league_code = X AND ingested_at = TIMESTAMP(Y))` pairs, per league. **Measured: the
  paired form costs 383,545,007 bytes on RAW_APIF_TRANSFERS, byte-identical to the unpaired form**,
  so partition pruning is unaffected and correctness was free. A test asserts the unpaired
  `s.ingested_at IN (` form cannot reappear.

- (the fake that could not express the bug) `test_missing_tables_do_not_raise` always supplied a
  `reference_season`, so the PLAYERS block was always present and the test could never reach the
  unguarded path; and `_FakeClient` had one has_tables flag, so it could not say "PLAYERS
  specifically is absent". Both were rebuilt: the fake now takes `missing_tables` per entity, and
  three tests cover PLAYERS-absent, all-absent, and one-snapshot-absent.

- (coverage gap closed rather than noted) `platform-reviewer` observed that the expected-set fix
  lived in an inline dict comprehension in the orchestrator, which no test exercised, so a future
  regression back to `selected_competitions()` would not be caught. Extracted to
  `per_team_expectations_from_results` in `completeness.py` and tested directly.

- (verification, round 2) Full suite **576 passed**. Re-verified against production after the query
  shape changed: 26 leagues judged, PLAYERS 0, SQUADS 0, TRANSFERS 0 missing, COACHES 29, GATED set
  EMPTY, ACN reference season 2027.

- (⭐ ROUND 2 FAILED ON BOTH SPECIALISTS, converging on ONE finding, and it is about my ARTIFACTS
  rather than my code)

  **`per_team_expectations_from_results` had ZERO test coverage.** In round 2 I extracted it out of
  the orchestrator specifically to close a `platform-reviewer` coverage gap, wrote "Now tested
  directly" into both this log and the contract, and never wrote the test. `grep -rn` over `tests/`
  returned nothing. So the regression the extraction exists to prevent — reverting to a
  `selected_competitions()` or `RAW_APIF_TEAMS`-derived expected set, the designs that reported 33
  false-missing pairs and ACN as 24/24 — would have shipped through the suite undetected. The fix
  moved the risk into a named function without closing it. Seven tests now cover it, including
  poll-mode exclusion and `max(seasons_list)`.

  Two further artifact inaccuracies found in the same pass:
  - `_ts_in_list` was DEAD CODE, left from the rejected unpaired-timestamp design, and the contract
    listed it among functions "each with one call site" when it had none. Deleted.
  - The contract's TABLES READ and cost derivation both described `RAW_APIF_TEAMS` as scanned. The
    shipped code never queries it; teams come from the in-memory run results. Stale copy from the
    rejected design.

  ⚠ THE LESSON, and it is the through-line of this whole task: **THREE separate statements in my
  contract were false, and every one was written in the same edit as the change it described.** The
  claim ran ahead of the code each time. Round 1's version said the NotFound guard covered all four
  tables when it covered three. Round 2's said a function was tested when no test existed. The
  fix is not "be more careful"; it is that a contract sentence asserting a property of the code —
  tested, guarded, has one call site, is read — must be verified against the code by grep or by a
  run BEFORE it is written down, exactly as `impact_map` evidence already must be. I verified the
  measurements in this task rigorously and the prose describing them not at all.

- (the cost figure re-measured against the SHIPPED code, not a design) Four queries per run: three
  maxima at 15,021 + 14,757 + 20,678 bytes and one coverage query at 999,200,648 bytes. **TOTAL
  999,251,104 bytes = 0.931 GiB, $0.0045 per run, $1.66 a year.** The earlier ~1.0 GiB figure was
  close but derived from shapes including a `RAW_APIF_TEAMS` scan the code does not perform.

- (verification, round 3) Full suite **583 passed**.

- (round 3 verdicts, and the FOURTH instance of the pattern) `data-engineer-reviewer` PASS,
  `platform-reviewer` PASS, `scope-auditor` FAIL. The FAIL was not a code defect: the contract said
  `evaluate_completeness_outcome` "gains one optional keyword argument" when it gains two
  (`per_team_missing`, `prior_per_team_missing`). Corrected by counting off the signature.
  ⚠ It was written INSIDE the amendment that documents this exact pattern. I stated the lesson
  accurately and repeated the error in the same edit, which says the lesson as prose does not bind
  even under full attention. The mechanical form is the one that would: a contract claim of the
  shape "X is tested / X is guarded / X has N callers / X is read / X gains N arguments" is a
  factual assertion about the tree and should be checked with a grep or a signature read at the
  moment it is written, the same way `impact_map` evidence already must be.

- (⭐ CPO RULING: THE ROUND CAP IS OVERRIDDEN) The cap of 3 was reached and the open finding was
  brought to the CPO rather than looped, per §2. The CPO authorised a FOURTH round. Recorded in
  `review.md` as `rounds_cap_override`, which is the mechanism the review template provides for
  exactly this. The round 4 diff differs from round 3 by one corrected sentence in `contract.md` and
  nothing else: no code, no test, no behaviour.

================================================================================
2026-08-06 — GitHub -> GitLab migration: guard status for the CI config
================================================================================

- (⭐ CPO RULING: .gitlab-ci.yml INHERITS THE GUARD STATUS OF .github/workflows/**)
  Context brought to the CPO: the GitHub account is suspended, CI has no host, and the six
  quality-gate workflows were being translated into a single `.gitlab-ci.yml`. The CI config is
  a guard — `.github/workflows/**` is BOTH a protected path (contract gate: needs
  `protected_override` + `impact_map`) AND a two-reviewer opus guard path (`cto-reviewer` +
  `platform-reviewer`). Its replacement matched NEITHER, so moving CI to a new host would have
  silently dropped two required reviewers and the override requirement from the one file that
  decides what CI enforces.

  The question was put with both options named: give `.gitlab-ci.yml` the same reviewers
  `.github/workflows/**` had, or leave it on `scope-auditor` only and accept lighter review on
  CI config than the GitHub workflows carried.

  CPO ANSWER: "Yes, match the old coverage." The routing row and the `PROTECTED_FILES` entry
  are that ruling applied to the same file for the same reason.

- (RECURRING COST, declared because widening a platform row is CPO-class) This adds
  `platform-reviewer` to a third guard path, so an MR touching `.gitlab-ci.yml` now spawns TWO
  opus specialists where the untranslated state would have spawned none (no routing row at all)
  and the GitHub equivalent spawned two. Net against the pre-migration baseline: unchanged —
  this restores the cost `.github/workflows/**` already carried rather than adding a new one.
  `review_routing.json`'s standing rule ("Do not widen them without a cost approval quoted in
  `decisions_taken`") is satisfied by the ruling above, quoted there.

- (SCOPE AMENDMENT, declared rather than made quietly) `.claude/task/escalations.log` and
  `.claude/task/TEMPLATE.md` were added to `scope_paths` AFTER the first review round, on
  `cto-reviewer`'s FAIL. Both were needed to fix findings that round raised: this file to give
  the ruling above a durable record, and TEMPLATE.md because it carries a fourth copy of the
  protected-path list that the change invalidates. The amendment is recorded here because a
  mid-task scope widening that only the contract mentions is the shape this log exists to catch.

- (round 1 verdicts) `scope-auditor` PASS. `cto-reviewer` FAIL, `platform-reviewer` FAIL — both
  found real defects, listed in `review.md`. The two FAILs are the reason this file has an entry
  at all: round 1's `protected_override` cited a CPO approval with no durable record, which
  `cto-reviewer` checked against this log and found absent.

- (round 2 verdicts, and the SAME pattern twice more) `cto-reviewer` FAIL, `platform-reviewer`
  FAIL. Both findings were stale RESTATEMENTS, not code defects, and both were in documents the
  round-1 sweep had declared complete.

  `platform-reviewer` found the contract's own `impact_map` still describing the removed
  fail-open design ("the data:* jobs are rules-gated on $GCP_SA_KEY … absent rather than failed")
  while `decisions_taken` #6, added in the same commit, said the opposite. The YAML comment was
  rewritten; this second copy of the same fact was not.

  `cto-reviewer` found two further guard-path COUNT restatements —
  `docs/roles/platform_reliability.md` and `docs/north_star.md` — missed because round 2's grep
  was scoped to the files already open rather than run across the repo.

  ⚠ THE LESSON, now recorded because it has fired three rounds running on one task: this change
  invalidates sentences on TWO axes (guard-path counts; protected-path enumerations) spread over
  seven and four files respectively, and each round's sweep was bounded by whatever was already
  in hand. A count or a list restated in prose has to be re-derived from the ROWS and swept
  REPO-WIDE. The 12-row CTO figure in this task was re-derived by counting the routing table
  programmatically rather than copied from the reviewer that reported it — the 2026-07-31 entry
  above records what taking a reviewer's arithmetic on trust cost last time ("Do not take my
  number on trust again — that is how one off-by-one became two documents").

- (SCOPE AMENDMENT, third) `docs/roles/platform_reliability.md` and `docs/north_star.md` added to
  `scope_paths` after round 2, on `cto-reviewer`'s FAIL. Same footing as the previous two: needed
  to fix findings that round raised, neither is a protected path, and the amendment is recorded
  here rather than only in the contract.

- (round 3 verdicts) `cto-reviewer` PASS, `platform-reviewer` PASS, `scope-auditor` ESCALATE. The
  two specialist FAILs from rounds 1-2 are cleared: the repo-wide sweep of both restatement axes
  holds, and the data jobs fail closed. The remaining open item is the scope question below.

- (⭐ CPO RULING: SCOPE AMENDMENT AUTHORITY FOR THE FOUR RESTATEMENT FILES) `scope-auditor`
  ESCALATED that `scope_paths` was amended four times mid-task — `escalations.log`, `TEMPLATE.md`,
  `docs/roles/platform_reliability.md`, `docs/north_star.md` — each time on a reviewer's FAIL, and
  that the three entries above cite that FAIL as the authority. The reviewer is RIGHT on the rule:
  `working_agreement.md:79` says an amendment records the CPO's authority, and a reviewer FAIL is
  not a substitute. This is the same line the same reviewer held on 2026-06-23 (entry at :145),
  where the CPO granted the amendment explicitly rather than letting reviewer-FAIL footing stand.

  Put to the CPO with both paths named: (a) confirm the amendment, or (b) revert the four files out
  of this branch to a follow-up MR — which would merge `.gitlab-ci.yml` with the guard counts
  knowingly stale in four documents, one of them `platform_reliability.md`, which is fed to
