- (⭐ CPO DECISION: DO NOT CREATE THE NIGHTLY SCHEDULE YET) The CPO reasoned it out rather than
  being asked: **"As long as we show no data, it doesn't make sense to schedule the nightly,
  right?"** Correct, and the builder confirmed it with one caveat rather than simply agreeing.

  WHY IT HOLDS: nothing reads the data — the legacy MVP is retired and v2 is not public (blocked
  on the imprint question) — so a standing nightly is daily spend against zero consumption. And
  almost nothing is lost by waiting: staging reads only the LATEST snapshot per league, there are
  no dbt snapshots accumulating SCD2 history, and fixtures/results/player stats/transfers are all
  retrievable from the API for past dates.

  THE ONE CAVEAT, stated so it is not discovered later: SQUADS are not backfillable.
  `docs/data_contract.md:125` — the endpoint is current-only, so "historical per-edition
  membership is not reconstructable from it". Every un-run night is a period whose squad
  membership cannot be recovered. Already a known parked limitation (#477), so nothing currently
  consumed is lost; it simply does not accrue.

  CONSEQUENCE: `data:nightly` ships INERT. It also accepts a manual `web` trigger, so the CPO can
  refresh data on demand before working against real numbers — the intended use until the site
  goes public, at which point creating the schedule is one UI action and no code change.

- (⭐ CPO INSTRUCTION: RUN THE NIGHTLY — and what it cost to carry out) After Phase 3 merged,
  the builder proposed manually triggering `data:nightly` as the next step, since it was built
  and reviewed but never exercised. CPO, 2026-08-06: **"ok do it"**, and when told a blocking
  defect had been found first, **"you need something from me?"** — i.e. no blocking input
  required, proceed.

  Recorded because the instruction could NOT be carried out as the file stood, and the fix
  was a change to a protected file. `data:nightly` is reachable only by web dispatch (no
  schedule exists). On that same dispatch `data:build:main` matched `$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH ==
  $CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH` and ran `when: on_success` — automatically — because GitLab evaluates
  `changes:` as TRUE on non-push pipelines. So clicking "run pipeline" for a nightly would
  ALSO have started a full prod warehouse build, the third that day, immediately after the
  CPO had twice objected to that exact spend ("full prod warehouse build? this is
  expensive"). Branch `chore/gitlab-ci-manual-prod-build` fixes it: `data:build:main` becomes
  a BUTTON on a web dispatch and stays automatic on a push to main.

  ⚠ THE AUTHORITY IS NARROW AND SHOULD NOT BE READ WIDER. "ok do it" authorised RUNNING THE
  NIGHTLY. It did not authorise this specific edit. The edit's justification is that it is a
  precondition to executing that instruction without unrequested spend — not that the CPO
  approved it. Stated here because an over-broad reading of a CPO utterance is exactly what
  `scope-auditor` has failed twice on this migration.

- (⭐ §10 CLASSIFICATION, escalated by `scope-auditor` and RESOLVED WITHOUT THE CPO) The
  reviewer ESCALATED on whether narrowing when an expensive warehouse job auto-runs is a
  §10 cost decision reserved to the CPO, invoking the meta-rule that an ambiguous
  classification is itself the CPO's call. The builder CHALLENGED rather than forwarding,
  on one argument the escalation had not addressed: **§10's cost reservation is directional**
  — it exists to stop a builder COMMITTING the CPO to unapproved spend. This change cannot
  increase spend in any context (push identical, schedule unchanged, web auto -> button) and
  removes no capability. If §10 also covered changes that only ever REDUCE unrequested
  spend, the reservation would protect the outcome it exists to prevent, and fixing a
  money-wasting bug would need a ruling before it could be fixed.

  `scope-auditor` accepted the argument and re-issued PASS. NO CPO ATTENTION WAS SPENT.
  Recorded because the repo logs "premature §10 escalation" as its own anti-pattern, and
  because this CPO has twice said explicitly they do not want decisions of this size brought
  to them — which is a reason to be SURE a call is genuinely §10 before escalating, never a
  reason to hide one that is.

- (round 1 verdicts) `cto-reviewer` PASS, `scope-auditor` FAIL (the missing cost-instruction
  record, fixed by the entry above), `data-engineer-reviewer` FAIL.

  ⚠ THE DATA-ENGINEER FINDING IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED, and no static check caught it:
  `data:nightly`, `deploy:export` and `deploy:site-v2` were all written `<<: *python` and all
  call `*gcp_auth`, but GitLab populates `$GITLAB_OIDC_TOKEN` ONLY for a job that declares
  `id_tokens:` itself — it is not inherited from a `default:` block and not implied by expanding
  the auth anchor. All three would have died on `.gcp_auth`'s third guard before doing any work.
  The nightly would have failed EVERY night once a schedule existed. `glab ci lint` passed it,
  the YAML parsed, and both new tests passed — because the config was well-formed and simply
  wrong, which is the same shape as the profile-location defect on MR !4.
  FIXED by extracting a `.gcp_job` anchor holding `id_tokens`, merged into every job that expands
  `*gcp_auth` (including `.data_build_base`, so there is now ONE definition rather than a copy
  per job), and pinned by `test_every_job_using_gcp_auth_declares_id_tokens`, which was verified
  to FAIL against the broken form before being accepted.

## 2026-08-06 — `review_summarise_paths`: a NEW MECHANISM in the review machinery

  RAISED BY `cto-reviewer` AT OPUS, as a FAIL on `chore/wire-the-unwired-guards`, and it was
  right. The builder had declared the mechanism honestly in `decisions_taken` but then asked the
  CTO REVIEWER to rule on it. That is the wrong party: §10 reserves new mechanisms to the CPO,
  and §2 step 3 says a reviewer never approves a §10 decision. Recorded here because the failure
  is reusable — declaring a crossing is not the same as getting it approved, and a reviewer
  cannot convert one into the other.

  THE SCOPE GAP. The approved plan said "`review_exclude_paths` already exists and simply does
  not cover `site_v2/src/data/**`" — one entry in an existing list. What was built is a THIRD
  routing key plus a manifest emitter in `git_discipline.py`, which changes what every reviewer
  is handed in every future task.

  WHY IT WAS BUILT BIGGER. Adding the paths to `review_exclude_paths` would have hidden the
  sample data outright. `bi-analyst-reviewer`'s core hunt item is whether a field displayed by a
  component actually EXISTS in the exported sample — a check performed by opening those files.
  Hiding them trades a payload saving for a real loss of coverage, which is the "never loosen a
  guard" failure. So the new key excludes them from the patch BODY and substitutes a MANIFEST
  (file list + `--stat` counts + an instruction to grep them). Reviewers keep `Read`/`Grep`, so
  the check survives; only the paste goes away.

  MEASURED, not asserted: 30,480 of 34,074 patch lines (89%) on the last task before this were
  committed sample JSON under `site_v2/src/data/**`.

  CPO ANSWER (2026-08-06): APPROVED, the manifest version. Put to him in plain language —
  reviewers are handed a file of which ~9 lines in 10 are machine-generated data nobody reads;
  the approved plan said delete those lines; deleting them outright also stops one reviewer
  knowing the data changed at all; the built version deletes them and leaves a note. His
  instruction: *"do as recommended"*, against a stated recommendation that the alternative is
  "cheaper to approve and strictly worse".

  BOUNDED: this approves `review_summarise_paths` as a mechanism and `site_v2/src/data/**` as its
  only member. Adding a path to that list later is a fresh decision — it removes content from
  what reviewers read, and the whole point of the key is that such removals are visible.

  NOT APPROVED, WITHDRAWN IN THE SAME REVIEW: an `i18n:same-as-en` exemption marker for
  `check_copy_gate.py`. `cto-reviewer` showed the case it existed for is already exempt (check 4
  skips values with no `[a-z]{3,}` word and single capitalised tokens), that the repo has zero
  instances needing it, and that the one historical case was settled by the CPO TRANSLATING the
  string with no exemption available. A self-serve comment would have let any future agent green
  a §10 copy check on its own authority. Removed; the gate's message now points at the CPO
  instead of advertising a hatch that does not exist.

  ALSO FROM THAT REVIEW, both builder defects, both fixed: the contract declared "RECURRING COST
  — NO" while moving `scope-auditor` haiku -> sonnet, which is a permanent per-commit increase
  (the approval existed; the declaration was false); and the new fail-open canary printed its
  JSON to stdout in CLI mode, appending a blob into `review_input.patch` itself. The second was
  reproduced before being fixed.

2026-08-07 test/1-guard-path-doc-parity — GitLab #1: derive the guard-path count and the
protected-path list from their sources, and test the eleven prose restatements against them.
LOGGED HERE PER THE 2026-06-17 RULING AT :112 — *"Path A — LOG IT. Up-front CPO design approvals
are recorded in escalations.log alongside review-time rulings; the contract's decisions_taken is
not, by itself, the sole record."* `scope-auditor` FAILed round 1 for exactly that omission: the
contract declared a NEW MECHANISM citing only a quoted instruction, with no entry here. The
reviewer was right, its citation was checked against :112 before being accepted, and this entry
is the fix.

- (⭐ NEW MECHANISM — prose must agree with the code it describes) The repo enforces a great deal
  over code and nothing over the PROSE describing it. `.claude/review_routing.json` and
  `task_contract_gate.py` are the sources for the guard-path count and the protected-path list;
  both are restated by hand across eleven sites. MR !4 added ONE routing row and that one-line
  change cost eleven prose edits and three review rounds, because nothing can tell you a
  hand-copied list is incomplete — a stale "eight" is valid prose.

  CPO INSTRUCTION (task prompt carried into this session, 2026-08-07), verbatim: *"Do #1 first and
  mechanically: derive the counts/lists from .claude/review_routing.json and
  task_contract_gate.py:PROTECTED_FILES, and add a test that fails when a prose site disagrees
  with the source. That is the pattern worth establishing; #19's full compression pass is its own
  session afterwards."* And, on the evidence bar: *"Verify the test FAILS against the broken form
  before accepting it — I shipped two vacuous tests in one MR yesterday and reviewers caught
  both."* Seven deliberate breaks were run and every one produced a red test.

  The plan implementing it was stated back and approved in plan mode before any file was written.

  BOUNDED: this authorises the parity TEST and nothing else. It edits no prose, no guard and no
  routing row, and adds no CI job. #19's compression pass is a separate session by the same
  instruction.

- (⭐ CPO RULING: the incomplete list the new test found is NOT fixed in this task) On its first
  run the test found that `.claude/agents/platform-reviewer.md:13-16` states that reviewer's
  territory and omits `.gitlab-ci.yml`, which routing DOES give it and which line 31 of the same
  brief lists. The 2026-08 migration updated the model-tier blockquote and missed the territory
  sentence six lines above. It is a TWELFTH restatement site; GitLab #1 counted eleven.

  Put to the CPO with both paths named: (a) fix the line here under a `protected_override`, which
  converts a tests-only diff into a governance change needing an `impact_map` and `cto-reviewer` at
  opus; or (b) record it, file it, and fix it in its own task. Builder recommended (b), on the
  reasoning that routing already summons the reviewer, so the ENFORCEMENT is correct and only the
  wording is wrong.

  CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-08-07): **(b)** — *"go ahead as recommended"*. Filed as GitLab
  #22. Pinned in the test as a `KNOWN_INCOMPLETE` entry paired with
  `test_known_incomplete_lists_have_not_been_fixed`, which goes RED the moment the prose is
  corrected, so the exemption cannot outlive the defect it excuses.

- ALSO FILED, incidental and out of scope: GitLab #23 — `.gitlab-ci.yml:363` still says Pages
  serves the MVP "until the parity cutover (#377)". That product was retired 2026-07-21 and there
  is no cutover. Same class as #1, on a protected path, so it needs its own governance task.

- REVIEWERS: `platform-reviewer` PASS round 1 — it re-derived all four counts independently from
  both sources and hit the same line-break trap the test's own docstring names, which is the check
  landing rather than a courtesy. `scope-auditor` FAIL round 1 on the missing entry above.

2026-08-07 docs/19-compile-dont-append — GitLab #19: three passages MOVED here out of
`docs/working_agreement.md` §2, verbatim in substance, so the rule document states rules and this
log keeps the record. Nothing is deleted; §2 now carries a citation to this entry in each place.
These three were the ONLY surviving copy — checked entry by entry against this log and against
`review_routing.json` before the move, unlike the other five passages compressed in the same task,
which were already recorded and so were simply dropped.

- (WHY `acceptance_criteria` EXISTS — moved from §2, CPO ruling 2026-07-31, #868) Every reviewer
  checks that the code is RIGHT, and nothing checked that it does WHAT WAS ASKED. A finished page
  passed both of its reviewers while opening on the wrong season. The ruling recorded above under
  the #868 org rulings ("the builder drafts, the CPO approves BEFORE any code") is the answer to
  that specific failure, and this sentence is the failure it answers.

- (WHY AN `Artifact` PUBLISH IS GATED — moved from §2, 2026-07-22) Design was the only surface in
  the repo with NO gate at all: three player-page mocks were produced and rejected in a single day
  without a contract, a routed reviewer, or a commit gate ever seeing them. Every path-keyed check
  returned early because a mock is written outside the repo. Hence the rule that an `Artifact`
  publish is denied unless a contract exists carrying a real `decisions_reserved`. The honest limit
  is stated in §2 and stays there because it is operative: this is keyed on the TOOL NAME, not on
  the act of designing, so drafting to a scratchpad is still ungated.

- (WHY A PROTECTED PATH NEEDS AN `impact_map` AS WELL AS AN OVERRIDE — moved from §2, 2026-07-22)
  Until that date only `protected_override` was required. That meant editing a GUARD demanded LESS
  evidence than a cosmetic label change on a leaf mart, while a guard's blast radius is every
  future task in the repo. The two answer different questions — the override answers "may you", the
  map answers "do you know what breaks" — and that half is a rule, so it stays in §2.

- (⭐ CPO RULING: AUTHORITY FOR THE METHOD CHANGE ABOVE) `scope-auditor` FAILED round 1 and was
  RIGHT. The approved plan's method was "remove a passage only where its counterpart already
  exists; anything else STAYS". The three moves above extend that to "move it here, then remove",
  and the builder wrote its own justification into `contract.md` citing the failure message of a
  test written in the same task. That is circular, and §2 says extending a contract without
  recorded CPO authority is drift by definition. No amount of good reasoning by the builder
  substitutes for the ruling.

  Put to the CPO with both paths named: (a) authorise the move — nothing is deleted, §2 keeps a
  citation at each site, compression stays at 12.2%; or (b) revert it, which returns all three to
  §2 and then forces a choice between shipping the new test RED or deleting the marker that
  catches one of them, the second being a guard weakened to fit the builder's own document.
  Builder recommended (a), on the ground that GitLab #19 itself asked for "nothing deleted, only
  moved to git + escalations.log" and that the narrowing to removal-only was the BUILDER's, in its
  plan, not the CPO's.

  CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-08-07): **(a) authorise the move** — *"go ahead as recommended"*.
  This entry is the authority `contract.md`'s `amendments:` block cites; the block previously cited
  nothing, which is the defect the reviewer named.

- (⭐ CPO RULING: THE NARRATIVE TEST IS WITHDRAWN) The guard this task was planned around was built
  and then removed. `platform-reviewer` FAILed it in rounds 1, 2 and 3, each round producing a
  legitimate RULE sentence the test would have wrongly rejected:
    r1 `was failed`      "a round that was failed on a paperwork-only nit still counts toward the cap"
    r1 `before that,`    "before that, confirm no artifact-only fix is possible"
    r2 `it ran on`       "a review's lock is void if it ran on a hash that predates the latest push"
    r2 `round N of the`  "a FAIL raised in round 2 of the cap must be fixed before round 3 opens"
    r3 `rounds N found`  "a defect that round 2 found is not re-litigated in round 3"
    r3 `until that date` "an override may declare an expiry; the edit stays permitted until that date"
  Six collisions, three rounds. Every fix was correct and every round surfaced more. Round 3 hit
  ROUND_CAP, so the builder STOPPED and brought the open findings to the CPO, which is what §2
  requires rather than a fourth patch.

  ROOT CAUSE, recorded so #26 does not restart from the same premise: rules and history are written
  in the SAME ENGLISH, and the failure is ASYMMETRIC. A missed narrative waits for a reviewer, which
  is a reviewer's job anyway; a false hit fires on a real RULE, and the cheapest way to green the
  build is to REWORD THE RULE. A keyword matcher therefore damages the document it protects.

  Put to the CPO as two paths: (A) ship the test with the two contested markers dropped, needing a
  round-cap override; or (B) ship the compression alone and make the guard its own work. The builder
  first recommended (A), then CORRECTED itself to (B) before he answered — recorded rather than
  smoothed over, because the first recommendation was anchored on the work already done rather than
  on the evidence, and he had asked for a plain-language explanation precisely because the earlier
  framing was unusable.

  CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-08-07): **(B)** — withdraw the test, ship the compression alone.
  `tests/test_governance_doc_parity.py` is reverted to `main`; `tests/` leaves `scope_paths`. Filed
  as GitLab #26.

  WHY THIS ENTRY EXISTS AT ALL: `scope-auditor` FAILed round 4 because the second amendment claimed
  this ruling in contract prose with nothing in this log — the SAME defect it failed round 1 for on
  the first amendment. The round-1 fix was applied to that amendment only and not to the class. It
  was right both times.

